findings.new:
**Assessment**, *three forces*. Accuracy, epistemic support and obligation (to investigate, disclose, perform or compel) have different grounds: content and the relation it concerns fix accuracy without an external commander, and neither other force follows.

<a id="r-learning-a-rule-by-narrowing"></a>
**Theorem** (any type of questions; candidate rules listed, observations and their acquisition supplied; `Becoming.Narrowing`, ported from StandardsBridge, empty axiom reports). Narrowing a list of candidate rules by observations keeps exactly the rules fitting them, the actual rule among them where it is listed and the observations are faithful, and a dropped rule contradicts some observation. The answer asserting what every kept rule agrees on is safe; on a nonempty candidate list a definite answer is error-free across the list only where every candidate agrees, so it is the greatest safe answer. Observations acquired from a listed actual rule make every asserted answer its value; one observed case leaves the other unsettled and contrasting cases settle both; a repertoire omitting the actual rule can answer confidently and falsely. Lean: `Narrowing.narrowed_iff_fits`, `Narrowing.actual_retained`, `Narrowing.answer_iff`, `Narrowing.safe_definite_requires_agreement`, `Narrowing.answer_maximal`, `Narrowing.acquired_answers_correct`, `Narrowing.wrong_model_class_can_be_confidently_wrong`;
